    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's what I implemented for the membership-level ceiling.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec: a new owner's `membershipLevel` may exceed the highest level already held among their household members by at most one; with no existing household member, no cap applies.\n\n**Where the logic lives**\n\n- **`model/MembershipLevel.java`** \u2014 added two small static helpers next to the existing derivation:\n  - `cappedToHousehold(level, householdMaxLevel)` \u2192 `min(level, householdMaxLevel + 1)` (the ceiling arithmetic).\n  - `effective(owner)` \u2192 returns the level fixed at creation time if recorded, else the derived level (so legacy/seed owners with no stored value still work).\n- **`rest/function/owner/AssignOwnerMembershipLevel.java`** (new pipeline step) \u2014 computes the owner's own derived level, finds the max *effective* level among existing household members (same `householdId`), applies the cap only when a member exists, and records the result. It runs after `AssignOwnerHouseholdSize`/`AssignOwnerNamesakeCount` (which feed the points) and before `SaveOwner`, following the existing `Assign*` convention.\n- **`model/Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `membershipLevel` field/column so the capped value is persisted and returned on later `GET`s (matching how `householdSize`/`namesakeCount` are frozen at creation).\n- **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** and **`AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 now read `MembershipLevel.effective(owner)`, so the response DTO and audit log report the capped value.\n- Wired the new step into `owners.POST.yml`; added `membership_level INTEGER` to all four DB schemas (h2, hsqldb, mysql, postgres).\n\n**One necessary behavioral change:** the acceptance test creates two owners in the same household (same lastName + postcode) without opting in via `sharesHousehold`, and expects both to succeed. The old `RequireUniqueIdentity` hard-rejected household co-members (409) unless they opted in. Since the level ceiling is the new mechanism governing multiple household members, I relaxed `RequireUniqueIdentity` to admit household co-members while still rejecting an exact identity duplicate (same telephone/email/household). No existing test covered the old household-409 behavior, and the full suite (`Tests run: 6, Failures: 0`) is green.\n\nI flag that last point explicitly because it goes slightly beyond the literal wording of the spec \u2014 it was required for the new test's two household members to be creatable at all. Let me know if you'd instead prefer household co-membership to remain opt-in (keeping the 409), in which case the test would need `sharesHousehold` set.",
